/-!
# The sheaves of differential forms with a logarithmic pole along a divisor

Let `f : X ⟶ S` be a morphism of schemes and let `Y ⊆ X` be an effective Cartier divisor, with
ideal sheaf `ℐ ⊆ 𝒪_X`. This file defines, for every `p`, the sheaf

`Ω^p_{X/S}(log Y) = SheafHom_{𝒪_X}(ℐ, 𝒦^p)`, where `𝒦^p = ker(Ω^p_{X/S} ⟶ i_*Ω^p_{Y/S})`,

together with the canonical inclusion of the forms with no pole.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.formRestrictionKernel`: the subsheaf `𝒦^p` of `Ω^p_{X/S}` of
  forms restricting to zero on the divisor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.logPolesForms`: the sheaf `Ω^p_{X/S}(log Y)`.

## Implementation notes

The usual presentation of `Ω^p_{X/S}(log Y)` fixes a local equation `t` of `Y` on an affine open
and takes the `𝒪`-submodule of `Ω^p_{X/S}(Y)` generated by `Ω^p_{X/S}` and
`dlog t ∧ Ω^{p-1}_{X/S}`, then glues. That is what the source does, and the gluing datum for it
is `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/DeRham/LogPoles/EquationDescent.lean`; its obstruction is that
the hypotheses of [Stacks, Tag 0FMV] hold at the *stalks* and so are available on no open.

The presentation here fixes no equation. On an open where `ℐ` is generated by a nonzerodivisor
`t` the evaluation `φ ↦ φ(t)` identifies `SheafHom(ℐ, 𝒦^p)` with `t^{-1}𝒦^p`, and `𝒦^p` is
`t Ω^p + dt ∧ Ω^{p-1}` — that is the content of
`Stacks.LogPoles.ker_mapExteriorPower_quotient_succ` together with
`Stacks.LogPoles.logSubmodule_span_singleton`, read at a stalk — so `t^{-1}𝒦^p` is
`Ω^p + dlog t ∧ Ω^{p-1}`, the sheaf the source constructs. Three things come for free that the
equation-wise presentation has to prove: the definition is independent of the equation, it is
uniform in `p` (in degree zero `𝒦^0 = ℐ` and `SheafHom(ℐ, ℐ)` is `𝒪_X`), and the second
component of `φ(t) = t ω + dt ∧ η` — the residue — does not move when `t` is replaced by `u t`.

`𝒦^p` is the `SheafOfModules.Submodule.ker` of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.formRestriction` rather than the abstract `Limits.kernel`, so that
its sections over an open are *by definition* the forms whose restriction to the divisor
vanishes, which is what the constructions of the inclusion, the residue and the differential
evaluate. The stalk is reached from the other spelling: `SheafOfModules.Submodule.kerIsoKernel`
identifies `(ker φ).toSheafOfModules` with `Limits.kernel φ`, whose stalk is the kernel of the
stalk map by `Stacks.SheafOfModules.TensorStalkRingedSpace.kernelStalkLinearEquiv`.
`SheafOfModules.Submodule.exact_ker_shortComplex` makes that identification usable on the
submodule spelling directly: it states that `ker φ → M → N` is an exact short complex, which is
the form in which the stalkwise statements about kernels are phrased, and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.TensorStalkRingedSpace.submoduleKerStalkLinearEquiv` is the resulting
linear equivalence over a ringed space.

Those are instantiated at `formRestriction` in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.DeRham.LogPoles.FormRestrictionKernelStalk`, which identifies the
stalk of `𝒦^p` with the kernel of the stalk of `formRestriction`. Which of the repository's
spellings of a sheaf of modules on `X` the statement is read in matters there, and the reason is
worth recording. The sheaves here live in `X.Modules`, whose sheaf of rings is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.ringCatSheaf`, that is
`(sheafCompose _ (forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat)).obj X.sheaf`. Unfolding that presents the
underlying presheaf of rings as `(sheafToPresheaf _ CommRingCat).obj X.sheaf ⋙ forget₂ _ _`, and
that is the term `PresheafOfModules.stalkMapL` infers from the type of a morphism of `X.Modules`;
so a statement written with `PresheafOfModules.stalkMapL` asks instance search for the module
structure on the stalk at that scalar spelling, which neither
`Stacks.ModuleStalk.stalkModule` nor
`Stacks.RelativeDifferentialStalkMap.moduleStalkRelativeDifferentialForms` is stated at — both
name the scalar `X.presheaf.stalk x`. The failure is in the elaboration of the statement, before
any proof begins, so a `letI` in a proof body does not reach it.

Naming the sheaves at the ringed-space spelling avoids the question rather than bridging it:
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.TensorStalkRingedSpace.structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace` has
`X.presheaf ⋙ forget₂ _ _` as its presheaf of rings already,
`Stacks.SchemeModulesSpelling.ofModules` is the identity from `X.Modules` to sheaves of modules
over it, and the module structure on the stalk is then found as
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.TensorStalkRingedSpace.stalkModule`. That structure and Mathlib's are
equal by `Stacks.SchemeModulesSpelling.stalkModule_eq`, which is how a consumer reading stalks in
the `X.presheaf.stalk x` spelling — [Stacks, Tag 0FMV] and `GlobalResidueSequence.lean` do —
crosses back.

`Stacks.Divisors.negativeTwist` is the ideal sheaf `ℐ` in the same spelling that
`Stacks.Divisors.invertibleSheaf` is the dual of. It is invertible, hence of finite presentation,
so the stalk of `SheafHom(ℐ, -)` is the module of linear maps between the stalks by Tag 01CP,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.sheafHomStalkLinearEquivOfFinitePresentation`; that is what makes the
degreewise short exactness of 0FMW a stalkwise statement over this middle term.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FMW](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FMW)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FMV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FMV)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory Opposite TopologicalSpace

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom

variable {X S : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ S) (I : X.IdealSheafData)

/-- The subsheaf `𝒦^p ⊆ Ω^p_{X/S}` of relative `p`-forms whose restriction to the closed
subscheme cut out by `I` vanishes. -/
def formRestrictionKernel (p : ℕ) :
    (relativeDifferentialForms f (p : ℤ)).Submodule :=
  _root_.SheafOfModules.Submodule.ker (formRestriction I.subschemeι f (p : ℤ))

/-- A local `p`-form lies in `𝒦^p` exactly when it restricts to zero on the divisor. -/
theorem mem_formRestrictionKernel (p : ℕ) (U : (X.Opens)ᵒᵖ)
    (ω : (relativeDifferentialForms f (p : ℤ)).val.obj U) :
    ω ∈ (formRestrictionKernel f I p).obj U ↔
      PresheafOfModules.Hom.app (formRestriction I.subschemeι f (p : ℤ)).val U ω = 0 :=
  _root_.SheafOfModules.Submodule.mem_ker _ _ _

/-- The sheaf `𝒦^p` of forms vanishing on the divisor, as a sheaf of `𝒪_X`-modules. -/
abbrev formRestrictionKernelSheaf (p : ℕ) : X.Modules :=
  (formRestrictionKernel f I p).toSheafOfModules

/-- **The sheaf `Ω^p_{X/S}(log Y)` of relative `p`-forms with a logarithmic pole along the
divisor `Y` cut out by `I`**: the sheaf of `𝒪_X`-linear maps from the ideal sheaf of `Y` to the
sheaf `𝒦^p` of `p`-forms vanishing on `Y`.

On an open where `Y` is cut out by a nonzerodivisor `t`, evaluation at `t` identifies this with
`t^{-1}(t Ω^p_{X/S} + dt ∧ Ω^{p-1}_{X/S}) = Ω^p_{X/S} + dlog t ∧ Ω^{p-1}_{X/S}`. -/
def logPolesForms (p : ℕ) : X.Modules :=
  Stacks.SheafOfModules.OnSpace.sheafHom X.toRingedSpace
    (Stacks.Divisors.negativeTwist I) (formRestrictionKernelSheaf f I p)

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom

end
